# what an await holds for a handler alone is read in a landing pad, and the pads of a handler stand in a row just ahead of it, far behind the blocks that await.
# Between its block and its pad such a value lies unread, so its place in the part of the frame that is kept at suspensions is another's there:
# values that follow each other take turns in two places, however long the try is, and every suspension saves and every resumption restores that much less.
# Without that each has a word of its own: the frame here was 128 bytes, 96 of them kept, and large async functions kept three times what a suspension ever holds.
# async function waits(make, awaited) { let last = make(make); try { await awaited; last = make(make); .. eight times ..; return await awaited; } catch { return make(last); } }
# checkAllocation() forgets a spill on an edge into its gap, and finds every pad served.
# REQUIRES: not-windows
# RUN: allocate f1:CoroutineBody mechanisms=none
# CHECK-COUNT-9: 64 bits, gap
# CHECK-NOT: gap
# REQUIRES(PLACES): x86_64 not-windows
# RUN(PLACES): frame f1:CoroutineBody mechanisms=none
# PLACES: size = 64
# PLACES: s3 at -[[one]]
# PLACES-NEXT: s4 at -[[other]]
# PLACES-NEXT: s5 at -[[one]]
# PLACES-NEXT: s6 at -[[other]]
# PLACES-NEXT: s7 at -[[one]]
# PLACES-NEXT: s8 at -[[other]]
# PLACES-NEXT: s9 at -[[one]]
# PLACES-NEXT: s10 at -[[other]]
# PLACES-NEXT: s11 at -[[one]]
# PLACES: bytesKeptAtSuspensions = 40

